  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: cbc795acf92c1fb48bae236e4ba4d39f, type: 3}
  m_Name: Q04_Extrinsic_PType
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::PNJunctionLab.AssessmentQuestion
  questionId: q04_ptype
  topic: 1
  topicTag: 2. Extrinsic Doping (N & P)
  shortSummaryLabel: Mass-Action & P-Type
  promptText: Silicon (ni = 1.5e10 cm^-3 at 300 K) is doped with Boron (Group III) to NA = 1e16 cm^-3. What are the equilibrium carrier densities?
  options:
  - n ~ 1e16 cm^-3 (majority) and p ~ 2.25e4 cm^-3 (minority)
  - n = p = 1e16 cm^-3 from charge neutrality
  - p ~ 1e16 cm^-3 (majority holes) and n = ni^2 / p ~ 2.25e4 cm^-3 (minority e-)
  - p ~ 1.5e10 cm^-3 and n = 0
  correctIndex: 2
  explanation: Trivalent acceptors (B, Al, Ga, In) form majority holes (p ~ NA) and fixed - acceptor ions. Mass-action n*p = ni^2 reduces minority electrons to (1.5e10)^2 / 1e16 = 2.25e4 cm^-3.
  recommendedStation: Stations 2 & 3
